    "['interface [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [0, 0], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'unit [parameter2] disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]'}]]": 2,
    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable false', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable false\\n##\\n\\n(Note: In Juniper, \"no shutdown\" is equivalent to enabling the interface, which is done by setting \"disable\" to false. The interface naming convention is slightly different - \"GigabitEthernet1/0/0\" becomes \"ge-1/0/0\" in Juniper syntax.)', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable false'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Junos, enabling IPv6 on an interface is done by configuring `family inet6` under the interface\\'s unit.\\n2. Unlike Cisco where `ipv6 enable` is a standalone command, Juniper requires explicit configuration of the protocol family.\\n3. Since the original Cisco config had IPv6 enabled on both GigabitEthernet1/0/0 and GigabitEthernet2/0/0, both interfaces are included in the translation.\\n4. The Junos interface naming converts \"GigabitEthernet\" to \"ge\" and uses a slightly different numbering format.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Junos, enabling IPv6 on an interface is done by configuring `family inet6` under the interface\\'s unit.\\n2. Unlike Cisco where `ipv6 enable` is a standalone command, Juniper requires explicit configuration of the protocol family.\\n3. Since the original Cisco config had IPv6 enabled on both GigabitEthernet1/0/0 and GigabitEthernet2/0/0, both interfaces are included in the translation.\\n4. The Junos interface naming converts \"GigabitEthernet\" to \"ge\" and uses a slightly different numbering format.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 address [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:1::1/64', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Juniper configuration command for the given Cisco `ipv6 address` command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:1::1/64\\n##\\n\\n(Note: In Juniper, interface naming typically uses a format like `ge-1/0/0` instead of Cisco's `GigabitEthernet1/0/0`, and IPv6 configuration is specified under the `unit 0 family inet6` hierarchy.)\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:1::1/64'}]]": 1,
    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable false', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable false\\n##\\n\\nFor Juniper, the equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\" is \"disable false\" (which removes the disable setting, effectively enabling the interface). The command structure follows Juniper\\'s hierarchy with \"set interfaces <interface-name> disable false\". \\n\\nNote: If the interface was never explicitly configured as \"disable\" (shutdown) in Juniper, it would be up by default, and this command wouldn\\'t need to be applied. However, this is the direct translation of the Cisco command.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 disable false'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, enabling IPv6 on an interface is done by configuring `family inet6` under the interface's unit.\\n- The `ipv6 enable` Cisco command is equivalent to setting the IPv6 family in Juniper.\\n- The translation is applied to both interfaces (ge-1/0/0 and ge-2/0/0) since both had `ipv6 enable` in the original config.\\n- Juniper uses `ge-x/y/z` notation instead of Cisco's `GigabitEthernetx/y/z`.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, enabling IPv6 on an interface is done by configuring `family inet6` under the interface's unit.\\n- The `ipv6 enable` Cisco command is equivalent to setting the IPv6 family in Juniper.\\n- The translation is applied to both interfaces (ge-1/0/0 and ge-2/0/0) since both had `ipv6 enable` in the original config.\\n- Juniper uses `ge-x/y/z` notation instead of Cisco's `GigabitEthernetx/y/z`.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 address [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:100::1/96', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `ipv6 address` command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:100::1/96\\n##\\n\\nThis command:\\n1. Selects the GigabitEthernet2/0/0 interface (ge-2/0/0 in Juniper)\\n2. Configures unit 0 (required in Juniper)\\n3. Enables IPv6 family (inet6)\\n4. Sets the same IPv6 address and prefix length (2001:db8:100::1/96)\\n\\nNote that in Juniper, you would typically need additional configuration to enable the interface (similar to Cisco's `no shutdown`), but this translation specifically covers only the IPv6 address assignment command.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:100::1/96'}]]": 1,
